        </div>

        <!-- PROSE -->
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Most vocabulary practice fails for a boring reason: the learner
            knows what's coming. A worksheet with twenty words in order, a
            flashcard deck that always starts the same way, a "go around the
            room" question round — the brain can coast through all of these on
            recognition alone, without ever doing the harder work of pulling a
            word or a verb form out of memory under a bit of pressure.
          </p>
          <p>
            A spin wheel breaks that. Nobody — including the teacher — knows
            what's coming next. That small dose of unpredictability is enough to
            shift a drill from passive to active, and it works whether you're
            running a classroom of thirty or quizzing yourself alone at a
            kitchen table.
          </p>
          <p>
            This guide collects
            <strong>20+ specific spin wheel setups</strong> for language
            learning — vocabulary, verbs, pronunciation, and conversation — with
            exact entries you can copy in, ideas sorted by proficiency level,
            and a section for self-learners studying without a classroom at all.
            If you've never built a wheel before, our
            <a href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/spin-the-wheel-tool"
              >quick guide to what a spin the wheel tool is</a
            >
            covers the basics in under two minutes.
          </p>

          <div class="callout">
            <span class="callout-icon">💡</span>
            <div>
              <strong>The short version</strong>
              A spin wheel forces retrieval instead of recognition. Not knowing
              what's coming next is what makes a drill feel like a game instead
              of a chore — and it's also what makes it work better.
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- SECTION 1 -->
          <h2 id="why-spin-wheel-works">
            <span class="emoji-header">🧠</span>Why a Spin Wheel Works for
            Language Learning
          </h2>
          <p>
            Language acquisition depends heavily on retrieval practice —
            actively pulling a word, a form, or a structure out of memory,
            rather than just recognising it when you see it. That's the gap
            between "I understand this when I read it" and "I can actually use
            this when I need it," and it's the gap most self-study routines
            never close.
          </p>
          <p>
            A wheel forces retrieval because the learner genuinely doesn't know
            what's about to land. There's no way to pre-load an answer the way
            you can when you know question six is always about colours. For a
            deeper look at why randomized selection changes how people engage
            with a task, see
            <a
              href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/psychology-random-decision-making"
              >the psychology behind random decision making</a
            >.
          </p>

          <div class="stats-row">
            <div class="stat-card">
              <span class="stat-number">20+</span>
              <span class="stat-label"
                >Ready-to-use wheel configurations in this guide</span
              >
            </div>
            <div class="stat-card">
              <span class="stat-number">3</span>
              <span class="stat-label"
                >Proficiency bands covered, A1 through C2</span
              >
            </div>
            <div class="stat-card">
              <span class="stat-number">&lt;2min</span>
              <span class="stat-label"
                >Time to build and start using any wheel here</span
              >
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            There's a group-dynamics angle too. When a wheel could land on
            anyone, everyone in the room stays quietly engaged, mentally
            rehearsing an answer, because it might be their turn next. That's a
            different kind of attention than passively waiting through a fixed
            order — and it's one reason teachers who try a vocabulary wheel for
            a single lesson often keep using it for the rest of term.
          </p>

          <!-- SECTION 2 -->
          <h2 id="vocabulary">
            <span class="emoji-header">📚</span>Vocabulary Building Wheels
          </h2>
          <p>
            Vocabulary is where repetition gets tedious fastest — and where a
            wheel earns its keep fastest too. Six setups to try:
          </p>

          <div class="ideas-grid">
            <div class="idea-card c1">
              <div class="idea-num">01 / VOCABULARY</div>
              <h3>Definition on the Spot</h3>
              <p>
                Load target words. Whoever the spin lands on defines the word
                from memory, no notes.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Example entries:</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">ephemeral</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">tenacious</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">meticulous</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">benevolent</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c2">
              <div class="idea-num">02 / VOCABULARY</div>
              <h3>Translate Under Pressure</h3>
              <p>
                Load words in the target language. The player has five seconds
                to give the translation.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label"
                >🎡 Example entries (Spanish → English):</span
              >
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">madrugada</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">vergüenza</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">sobremesa</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c3">
              <div class="idea-num">03 / VOCABULARY</div>
              <h3>Use It in a Sentence</h3>
              <p>
                The wheel picks a word; the player builds one original, correct
                sentence with it in 30 seconds.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Level up:</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">Add a time limit</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Require two sentences</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c4">
              <div class="idea-num">04 / VOCABULARY</div>
              <h3>Category Blast</h3>
              <p>
                Load semantic categories. The wheel picks one; the group has 60
                seconds to name as many items as possible.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Example entries:</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">Kitchen items</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Body parts</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Emotions</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c1">
              <div class="idea-num">05 / VOCABULARY</div>
              <h3>Synonym Swap</h3>
              <p>
                Load common words; the player gives a synonym or antonym in the
                same language.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Example entries:</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">happy</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">big</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">difficult</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c2">
              <div class="idea-num">06 / VOCABULARY</div>
              <h3>Word of the Day Picker</h3>
              <p>
                Pre-load a week's vocabulary list. Spin each morning to pick
                that day's focus word.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Works well for:</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">Weekly wordlists</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Exam vocab sets</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="tip-box">
            <strong>💚 Tip:</strong> Turn on "remove after spin" for vocabulary
            wheels so every word gets covered before anything repeats. Useful
            for exam revision where full coverage matters more than repetition
            of the easy words.
          </div>

          <!-- SECTION 3 -->
          <h2 id="verbs">
            <span class="emoji-header">🔀</span>Verb Conjugation & Grammar
            Roulette
          </h2>
          <p>
            Conjugation punishes rote memorisation the moment it's tested out of
            sequence — which is exactly the situation a wheel creates on
            purpose. Four setups worth building:
          </p>
          <div class="ideas-grid">
            <div class="idea-card c3">
              <div class="idea-num">07 / GRAMMAR</div>
              <h3>Tense Roulette</h3>
              <p>
                Load tenses onto the wheel. Announce a verb, spin, and the
                player conjugates it in whatever tense lands.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Example entries (French):</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">Présent</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Passé composé</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Imparfait</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Futur simple</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c4">
              <div class="idea-num">08 / GRAMMAR</div>
              <h3>Subject + Verb Double Spin</h3>
              <p>
                Build two wheels — subjects on one, verbs on the other. Spin
                both; the player conjugates on the fly.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Wheel A (subjects):</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">je / io / ich</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">tu</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">nous</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">vous</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c1">
              <div class="idea-num">09 / GRAMMAR</div>
              <h3>Irregular Verb Spotlight</h3>
              <p>
                Load the most common irregular verbs. Spin picks one; the player
                gives all forms in a chosen tense from memory.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label"
                >🎡 Example entries (Spanish):</span
              >
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">ser</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">tener</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">ir</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">hacer</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c2">
              <div class="idea-num">10 / GRAMMAR</div>
              <h3>Structure Roulette</h3>
              <p>
                Load a grammar structure; the player builds two original example
                sentences with it.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Example entries (German):</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">Accusative case</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Separable verbs</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Passive voice</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Classes juggling more than one target language can add a "language
            roulette" wheel that decides which language the conjugation has to
            be in — a genuinely difficult but effective way to keep advanced
            groups on their toes.
          </p>

          <div class="info-box">
            <strong>ℹ️ Worth knowing:</strong> Mixing related-but-distinct items
            together — different tenses, different verbs — rather than drilling
            one at a time is called interleaved practice. It tends to feel
            harder in the moment than blocked, repetitive drilling, but produces
            sturdier long-term recall. A wheel is one of the easiest ways to
            interleave a drill without extra planning.
          </div>

          <figure>
            <img
              src="/eng-letter-picker-wheel.webp"
              alt="Illustration of two spin wheels side by side, one labelled with pronouns and one with verbs, representing a double-spin verb conjugation activity"
              width="1200"
              height="600"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption>
              Two wheels spun together — one for the subject, one for the verb —
              add genuine unpredictability to conjugation drills.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <!-- SECTION 4 -->
          <h2 id="conversation">
            <span class="emoji-header">💬</span>Conversation Starter Wheels
          </h2>
          <p>
            Speaking is the skill most learners struggle with longest, and it's
            rarely a vocabulary problem — it's that real conversation is
            unscripted, and most classroom speaking practice isn't. A
            conversation wheel is a safe way to practice the unscripted part.
          </p>
          <div class="ideas-grid">
            <div class="idea-card c4">
              <div class="idea-num">11 / SPEAKING</div>
              <h3>Topic Spinner</h3>
              <p>
                Load topics; the player speaks for 60–90 seconds on whatever
                lands, no prep, no notes.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Example entries:</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">My weekend</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Favourite food</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">My future plans</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c1">
              <div class="idea-num">12 / SPEAKING</div>
              <h3>Opinion Roulette</h3>
              <p>
                Load thought-provoking questions. The player answers with an
                opinion and at least one reason — good debate prep.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Example entries:</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">Should school be free?</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Is social media harmful?</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c2">
              <div class="idea-num">13 / SPEAKING</div>
              <h3>Role-Play Scenario Selector</h3>
              <p>
                Load classic exam-style scenarios; two students spin together
                and improvise a two-minute dialogue on whatever lands.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Example entries:</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">At the doctor's</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Job interview</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Restaurant complaint</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c3">
              <div class="idea-num">14 / SPEAKING</div>
              <h3>Constraint Wheel</h3>
              <p>
                Instead of topics, load constraints — "no fillers," "speak only
                in questions" — to add playful difficulty to any pair
                conversation.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Example entries:</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">Speak in questions only</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Use a metaphor</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="callout">
            <span class="callout-icon">🎯</span>
            <div>
              <strong>Double-spin combo</strong>
              Pair the conversation wheel with a name-picker wheel — first spin
              picks the topic, second picks who speaks. Because everyone could
              be picked for the topic already on screen, the whole group ends up
              rehearsing it in their head, not just the person who's eventually
              chosen.
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- CTA BANNER -->
          <div class="cta-banner">
            <h3>🎡 Build Your Language Wheel in 60 Seconds</h3>
            <p>
              Free, browser-based, no sign-up. Type your vocabulary, verbs, or
              topics — and spin.
            </p>
            <a href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/"
              >Open Spin the Wheel — Free →</a
            >
          </div>

          <!-- SECTION 5 -->
          <h2 id="pronunciation">
            <span class="emoji-header">🎙️</span>Pronunciation & Listening
            Practice
          </h2>
          <p>
            Pronunciation tends to get squeezed out of language curricula, but
            it's one of the most practically important skills for actually being
            understood. A wheel gives it a low-stakes, slightly gamified format
            instead of an isolated drill nobody enjoys.
          </p>
          <div class="ideas-grid">
            <div class="idea-card c1">
              <div class="idea-num">15 / PRONUNCIATION</div>
              <h3>Minimal Pairs Roulette</h3>
              <p>
                Load word pairs that differ by a single sound. The wheel picks
                one; the player pronounces it and uses it in context.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label"
                >🎡 Example entries (English learners):</span
              >
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">ship / sheep</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">bit / beat</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">think / sink</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c2">
              <div class="idea-num">16 / PRONUNCIATION</div>
              <h3>Tongue Twister Spinner</h3>
              <p>
                Load short, phonetically tricky phrases. The player says it
                clearly three times, faster each time.
              </p>
              <span class="idea-wheel-label">🎡 Good for:</span>
              <div class="idea-entries">
                <span class="entry-tag">French nasal vowels</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">Spanish r/rr</span
                ><span class="entry-tag">English th sounds</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="idea-card c3">
              <div class="idea-num">17 / LISTENING</div>
              <h3>Listen & Summarise Wheel</h3>
              <p>
                Load media types. The wheel picks one; play a short clip of that
                type, then summarise it in the target language.
